## 5. Risk factors

### 1. Salesforce Direct Competitive Risks
Salesforce launches dedicated Life Sciences Cloud, directly competing for Veeva's core customers. Although it has only acquired 3 of the top 20 pharmaceutical companies so far, Salesforce has strong sales capabilities and brand influence, and long-term competition cannot be ignored.

### 2. CRM platform migration execution risks
Veeva is migrating its CRM products from the Salesforce platform to its own Vault platform, with most of the migration expected to be completed between 2026-2029. The company has made it clear that it will not migrate custom code, custom objects or third-party integrations, which could lead to customer dissatisfaction and technical complexity.

### 3. Cyclicality of the life sciences industry
Veeva's revenue is highly dependent on research and development spending from the pharmaceutical and biotechnology industries. If the industry enters a down cycle (such as tightening of new drug approvals and worsening financing environment), customers may postpone purchases or cut spending.

### 4. Risks of AI disruption
The rapid development of large language models may change the landscape of the software industry. Morningstar has raised its uncertainty rating to "high" as a result. While Veeva's regulatory compliance barriers provide some protection, the impact of AI on software remains difficult to predict.

### 5. Data security and privacy supervision
Veeva handles large amounts of sensitive clinical and patient data and faces increasingly stringent global privacy and AI regulatory risks.

